1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,162
In just a few weeks,

2
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:03,825
AI claimed solutions to some of the hardest

3
00:00:03,838 --> 00:00:05,225
problems in mathematics.

4
00:00:05,638 --> 00:00:06,712
In one single night:

5
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:08,175
seven hundred results.

6
00:00:08,588 --> 00:00:09,862
One mathematician said:

7
00:00:10,312 --> 00:00:11,500
if a human had done this,

8
00:00:11,725 --> 00:00:13,388
it would be an instant Fields Medal.

9
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:16,212
Lex Fridman called it an emergency episode.

10
00:00:16,625 --> 00:00:18,312
This is that three and a half hour

11
00:00:18,325 --> 00:00:19,975
conversation in ten minutes.

12
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:21,975
What AI actually solved,

13
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:24,500
why some of the best mathematicians alive are

14
00:00:24,512 --> 00:00:26,525
in mourning, and the one thing a machine

15
00:00:26,538 --> 00:00:27,488
still can't give you.

16
00:00:27,979 --> 00:00:28,329
Part one.

17
00:00:28,754 --> 00:00:29,804
The night math changed.

18
00:00:30,217 --> 00:00:31,892
It's a Tuesday evening in New York.

19
00:00:32,304 --> 00:00:34,879
Rutgers number theorist Alex Kontorovich is at a

20
00:00:34,892 --> 00:00:37,304
gala at the National Museum of Mathematics,

21
00:00:37,579 --> 00:00:39,679
a whiskey in one hand and his phone

22
00:00:39,692 --> 00:00:40,254
in the other.

23
00:00:40,679 --> 00:00:41,579
He keeps refreshing,

24
00:00:41,842 --> 00:00:44,342
because OpenAI is about to release something around

25
00:00:44,354 --> 00:00:45,042
six o'clock.

26
00:00:45,467 --> 00:00:46,142
Then it drops.

27
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:48,129
Seven hundred solved problems.

28
00:00:48,542 --> 00:00:50,604
And the second thing he sees is quasi

29
00:00:50,617 --> 00:00:51,329
R H,

30
00:00:51,717 --> 00:00:53,979
a partial version of the Riemann hypothesis,

31
00:00:54,254 --> 00:00:56,379
the most famous open problem in his field.

32
00:00:56,804 --> 00:00:57,517
His first thought:

33
00:00:57,829 --> 00:00:59,254
that can't be what's in this drop.

34
00:00:59,679 --> 00:01:01,592
He says he simply could not compute what

35
00:01:01,604 --> 00:01:02,267
he was seeing.

36
00:01:02,679 --> 00:01:05,654
At the same gala is MIT's Ankur Moitra.

37
00:01:06,092 --> 00:01:07,867
For him it wasn't a total surprise.

38
00:01:08,317 --> 00:01:09,917
For weeks there had been rumors that the

39
00:01:09,992 --> 00:01:12,242
biggest problems in his area had fallen,

40
00:01:12,642 --> 00:01:14,579
including the Unique Games Conjecture.

41
00:01:15,004 --> 00:01:17,379
One colleague even rushed out his own paper,

42
00:01:17,679 --> 00:01:19,392
with a footnote saying that because of the

43
00:01:19,404 --> 00:01:21,767
rumors, he couldn't wait any longer.

44
00:01:22,192 --> 00:01:23,567
Just hours before the drop,

45
00:01:23,979 --> 00:01:25,604
Ankur asked Subhash Khot,

46
00:01:25,892 --> 00:01:28,354
the man who proposed the Unique Games Conjecture,

47
00:01:28,654 --> 00:01:30,492
whether he would read an AI proof of

48
00:01:30,504 --> 00:01:30,679
it.

49
00:01:31,092 --> 00:01:32,104
Khot's answer was simply:

50
00:01:32,467 --> 00:01:32,679
no.

51
00:01:33,104 --> 00:01:34,754
And this wasn't even the first shock.

52
00:01:35,167 --> 00:01:36,117
A few weeks earlier,

53
00:01:36,467 --> 00:01:38,479
Alex gave a talk at Princeton explaining that

54
00:01:38,567 --> 00:01:41,842
AI was still years away from formalizing Fermat's

55
00:01:41,854 --> 00:01:43,742
Last Theorem in the proof checker Lean.

56
00:01:44,167 --> 00:01:44,892
Two days later,

57
00:01:45,117 --> 00:01:46,904
Anthropic released exactly that.

58
00:01:47,379 --> 00:01:49,492
Thirteen million lines of verified code.

59
00:01:49,904 --> 00:01:51,454
As far as Alex understands,

60
00:01:51,704 --> 00:01:53,342
two or three people were involved,

61
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:55,229
and most of their input was typing:

62
00:01:55,717 --> 00:01:56,267
keep going.

63
00:01:56,679 --> 00:01:58,042
Then came Navier-Stokes.

64
00:01:58,479 --> 00:02:00,867
For Alex, the quasi Riemann night was round

65
00:02:00,879 --> 00:02:01,204
three.

66
00:02:01,712 --> 00:02:02,150
Part two.

67
00:02:02,562 --> 00:02:03,875
What AI actually solved.

68
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:05,712
Start with the prime numbers,

69
00:02:05,912 --> 00:02:07,512
the atoms of multiplication.

70
00:02:07,925 --> 00:02:09,712
Every whole number is built from them,

71
00:02:10,012 --> 00:02:12,150
but their pattern has always been a mystery.

72
00:02:12,575 --> 00:02:14,912
Bernhard Riemann found a function that works like

73
00:02:14,925 --> 00:02:16,825
signal processing for the primes.

74
00:02:17,237 --> 00:02:19,588
Alex compares it to a microphone turning your

75
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:21,212
voice into frequencies.

76
00:02:21,638 --> 00:02:24,075
The Riemann hypothesis says that in the music

77
00:02:24,088 --> 00:02:24,925
of the primes,

78
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:28,075
every instrument plays at exactly the same volume.

79
00:02:28,487 --> 00:02:29,438
In math terms:

80
00:02:29,825 --> 00:02:32,188
all the important zeros of this function sit

81
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:33,350
on one single line,

82
00:02:33,638 --> 00:02:34,738
the line one half.

83
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:36,087
For seventy years,

84
00:02:36,275 --> 00:02:38,325
the best anyone could prove was a zero

85
00:02:38,337 --> 00:02:40,500
free zone that got thinner and thinner.

86
00:02:40,912 --> 00:02:43,337
The AI proved an entire strip with no

87
00:02:43,350 --> 00:02:44,262
zeros at all,

88
00:02:44,562 --> 00:02:45,825
from seven eighths to one.

89
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:48,450
Push that seven eighths down to one half,

90
00:02:48,837 --> 00:02:50,800
and you have the full Riemann hypothesis.

91
00:02:51,212 --> 00:02:52,250
Alex is careful here.

92
00:02:52,675 --> 00:02:54,550
This method fundamentally can't get there.

93
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:56,225
A new idea is needed.

94
00:02:56,650 --> 00:02:58,138
But the proof is checked in Lean,

95
00:02:58,450 --> 00:02:59,938
so he is a hundred percent sure it's

96
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:00,425
correct.

97
00:03:00,837 --> 00:03:04,550
Then Navier-Stokes, the equations behind every fluid on

98
00:03:04,562 --> 00:03:04,975
Earth.

99
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:07,888
They are basically F equals m a for

100
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:08,938
water and air.

101
00:03:09,350 --> 00:03:10,638
The million dollar question:

102
00:03:11,012 --> 00:03:13,200
can a smooth flow suddenly blow up to

103
00:03:13,212 --> 00:03:13,750
infinity?

104
00:03:14,162 --> 00:03:17,675
University of Washington engineer Steve Brunton explains what

105
00:03:17,688 --> 00:03:20,112
the AI found for one version of the

106
00:03:20,125 --> 00:03:20,562
problem.

107
00:03:20,975 --> 00:03:24,288
A perfectly orchestrated vortex that keeps stretching and

108
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:26,675
thinning, like a figure skater pulling in their

109
00:03:26,688 --> 00:03:29,375
arms, spinning faster and faster until,

110
00:03:29,775 --> 00:03:30,475
at one point,

111
00:03:30,875 --> 00:03:31,825
it becomes infinite.

112
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:33,138
But here's the twist.

113
00:03:33,562 --> 00:03:36,212
Steve says almost nothing changed for engineers.

114
00:03:36,625 --> 00:03:39,112
Airplane wings and artificial hearts are just as

115
00:03:39,125 --> 00:03:40,925
hard to simulate as before.

116
00:03:41,350 --> 00:03:43,312
We solved the million dollar math problem,

117
00:03:43,675 --> 00:03:44,212
he says.

118
00:03:44,638 --> 00:03:47,262
The trillion dollar engineering problem is still open.

119
00:03:47,675 --> 00:03:49,087
And the Unique Games Conjecture?

120
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:51,338
It's a bet about which hard problems we

121
00:03:51,350 --> 00:03:52,412
can even approximate.

122
00:03:52,825 --> 00:03:53,500
If it's true,

123
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:56,288
it organizes a huge part of computer science.

124
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:58,712
Ankur admits he was one of the doubters.

125
00:03:59,125 --> 00:04:00,550
It sounded too nice to be true.

126
00:04:00,962 --> 00:04:01,425
It's true.

127
00:04:01,913 --> 00:04:02,425
Part three.

128
00:04:02,838 --> 00:04:04,188
Is AI really creative?

129
00:04:04,612 --> 00:04:06,475
Or is it just remixing what humans already

130
00:04:06,488 --> 00:04:06,838
wrote?

131
00:04:07,263 --> 00:04:08,438
The guests use a balloon.

132
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:12,000
Everything mathematicians have discovered is the inside of

133
00:04:12,013 --> 00:04:12,562
the balloon.

134
00:04:12,975 --> 00:04:15,600
If AI only combines what's already inside,

135
00:04:15,888 --> 00:04:17,488
critics call it the convex hull.

136
00:04:17,900 --> 00:04:18,650
Nothing really new.

137
00:04:19,062 --> 00:04:20,588
Alex prefers another picture,

138
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:21,775
from Tim Gowers:

139
00:04:21,975 --> 00:04:22,550
tiles.

140
00:04:22,975 --> 00:04:25,000
You keep placing known tiles next to each

141
00:04:25,012 --> 00:04:25,288
other,

142
00:04:25,675 --> 00:04:27,625
and nobody knows how far that can reach.

143
00:04:28,112 --> 00:04:29,262
For quasi R H,

144
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:31,825
every single move was known to humans.

145
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:34,238
Some mathematicians are kicking themselves.

146
00:04:34,650 --> 00:04:36,562
But the chain of moves was so long

147
00:04:36,688 --> 00:04:39,013
and so beautiful that Alex calls it shocking.

148
00:04:39,425 --> 00:04:40,938
Ankur adds something unsettling.

149
00:04:41,362 --> 00:04:43,275
These proofs just don't look like a human

150
00:04:43,288 --> 00:04:43,788
solved them.

151
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:46,625
It reminds him of image AI that recognizes

152
00:04:46,638 --> 00:04:49,200
cats using patterns humans can't even see.

153
00:04:49,612 --> 00:04:51,150
Another guest puts it more hopefully.

154
00:04:51,562 --> 00:04:52,950
Even if it's only remixing,

155
00:04:53,238 --> 00:04:54,138
it lifts the fog.

156
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:56,350
It's like giving mathematicians radar.

157
00:04:56,762 --> 00:04:58,000
And it redraws the map.

158
00:04:58,412 --> 00:05:00,688
An Anthropic result on a problem called three

159
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:02,562
sum, the kind of puzzle you get in

160
00:05:02,575 --> 00:05:05,888
coding interviews, turned out faster than almost anyone

161
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:06,862
believed possible.

162
00:05:07,275 --> 00:05:09,125
An entire theory had been built on that

163
00:05:09,138 --> 00:05:10,225
problem being hard.

164
00:05:10,638 --> 00:05:13,275
Now that whole field has to reorganize itself.

165
00:05:13,688 --> 00:05:14,250
There's a catch,

166
00:05:14,288 --> 00:05:14,525
though.

167
00:05:14,938 --> 00:05:16,650
The AI didn't win every time.

168
00:05:17,075 --> 00:05:18,125
According to Ankur,

169
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:20,762
thousands of problems were reportedly attempted,

170
00:05:21,138 --> 00:05:23,012
and only the successes were published,

171
00:05:23,362 --> 00:05:24,375
roughly one in ten.

172
00:05:24,788 --> 00:05:27,625
Nobody knows exactly where the line between solved

173
00:05:27,775 --> 00:05:29,012
and unsolved runs.

174
00:05:29,425 --> 00:05:30,412
As one guest puts it,

175
00:05:30,712 --> 00:05:33,200
they're readjusting to their inability to predict.

176
00:05:33,713 --> 00:05:34,238
Part four.

177
00:05:34,662 --> 00:05:36,075
Why mathematicians are mourning.

178
00:05:36,550 --> 00:05:38,713
Fields medalist Terence Tao wrote that he is

179
00:05:38,725 --> 00:05:40,638
horrified by years of patient,

180
00:05:40,950 --> 00:05:44,312
deliberately slow research by graduate students being casually

181
00:05:44,325 --> 00:05:44,912
disrupted.

182
00:05:45,325 --> 00:05:46,900
You can't unhear a movie spoiler.

183
00:05:47,312 --> 00:05:47,738
And then:

184
00:05:48,138 --> 00:05:49,500
I mourn the path not taken.

185
00:05:49,912 --> 00:05:50,825
Ankur feels it too.

186
00:05:51,238 --> 00:05:53,825
The old blueprint for young mathematicians was simple.

187
00:05:54,238 --> 00:05:55,812
Take a problem people care about,

188
00:05:56,175 --> 00:05:57,213
make progress on it,

189
00:05:57,513 --> 00:05:58,188
and doors open.

190
00:05:58,612 --> 00:05:59,638
That blueprint is gone.

191
00:06:00,062 --> 00:06:02,312
He says he feels more intellectually lonely than

192
00:06:02,325 --> 00:06:02,838
before.

193
00:06:03,263 --> 00:06:05,250
Some people now run scripts that scrape the

194
00:06:05,263 --> 00:06:07,900
web for open problems and flood the preprint

195
00:06:07,912 --> 00:06:09,513
servers with AI papers.

196
00:06:09,925 --> 00:06:11,338
And there's a darker side effect.

197
00:06:11,750 --> 00:06:14,200
Mathematicians may start hiding their ideas,

198
00:06:14,388 --> 00:06:16,175
because once an idea is public,

199
00:06:16,425 --> 00:06:18,562
an AI might finish the job in a

200
00:06:18,575 --> 00:06:19,312
single night.

201
00:06:19,725 --> 00:06:21,900
Alex tells the story of Andrew Wiles.

202
00:06:22,312 --> 00:06:24,638
Years after proving Fermat's Last Theorem,

203
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:26,738
Wiles sat in front of a camera and

204
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:29,325
tried to describe the moment he finally saw

205
00:06:29,338 --> 00:06:30,488
how to fix his proof.

206
00:06:30,912 --> 00:06:32,463
He said he would never again have a

207
00:06:32,475 --> 00:06:33,700
moment that powerful.

208
00:06:34,112 --> 00:06:34,825
And he choked up.

209
00:06:35,238 --> 00:06:36,800
Could you get that moment by pressing a

210
00:06:36,812 --> 00:06:37,150
button?

211
00:06:37,562 --> 00:06:38,338
Absolutely not,

212
00:06:38,688 --> 00:06:39,325
Alex says.

213
00:06:39,738 --> 00:06:41,475
It's like training for years to climb a

214
00:06:41,488 --> 00:06:41,900
mountain,

215
00:06:42,275 --> 00:06:44,288
and then a helicopter lands and drops you

216
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:44,838
at the top.

217
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:47,812
Lex compares it to Olympic champions who feel

218
00:06:47,950 --> 00:06:49,400
empty after winning gold,

219
00:06:49,788 --> 00:06:51,550
and to programmers who felt a loss of

220
00:06:51,562 --> 00:06:54,138
meaning when AI started writing most of their

221
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:54,500
code.

222
00:06:54,925 --> 00:06:56,075
But not everyone is mourning.

223
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:57,812
Steve is mostly hopeful.

224
00:06:58,225 --> 00:07:00,388
If another country invented teleportation,

225
00:07:00,775 --> 00:07:03,188
he says, we would reinvent it within five

226
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:03,700
years.

227
00:07:04,112 --> 00:07:06,575
Knowing a solution exists is also a huge

228
00:07:06,588 --> 00:07:07,238
motivation.

229
00:07:07,746 --> 00:07:08,283
Part five.

230
00:07:08,696 --> 00:07:10,021
What AI can't take from us.

231
00:07:10,433 --> 00:07:12,808
Ankur's answer starts with something called the doorman

232
00:07:12,821 --> 00:07:13,396
fallacy.

233
00:07:13,808 --> 00:07:16,096
If you think a doorman only opens doors,

234
00:07:16,358 --> 00:07:18,171
you replace him with an automatic door.

235
00:07:18,583 --> 00:07:19,858
But you lose everything else.

236
00:07:20,283 --> 00:07:21,933
The welcome, the recognition,

237
00:07:22,296 --> 00:07:24,908
the security, the feeling of being cared for.

238
00:07:25,321 --> 00:07:27,721
The visible part of a mathematician's job is

239
00:07:27,733 --> 00:07:28,721
proving theorems.

240
00:07:29,133 --> 00:07:29,971
But the real job,

241
00:07:30,358 --> 00:07:32,321
Ankur says, is thinking deeply.

242
00:07:32,733 --> 00:07:34,758
New theories, new connections,

243
00:07:35,008 --> 00:07:36,108
new ways of seeing.

244
00:07:36,533 --> 00:07:38,246
When problem solving becomes cheap,

245
00:07:38,646 --> 00:07:40,508
everything else becomes more valuable.

246
00:07:40,933 --> 00:07:42,521
So what's the one thing a machine still

247
00:07:42,533 --> 00:07:43,108
can't give you?

248
00:07:43,521 --> 00:07:44,858
Ankur tells it as a story.

249
00:07:45,271 --> 00:07:46,371
In the nineteen fifties,

250
00:07:46,571 --> 00:07:47,958
a mathematician wrote a play.

251
00:07:48,371 --> 00:07:49,746
John von Neumann dies,

252
00:07:50,096 --> 00:07:50,808
goes to heaven,

253
00:07:51,183 --> 00:07:52,996
and gets to ask God one question.

254
00:07:53,408 --> 00:07:54,796
Is the Riemann hypothesis true?

255
00:07:55,208 --> 00:07:55,996
God says yes.

256
00:07:56,408 --> 00:07:57,346
Everyone exhales.

257
00:07:57,758 --> 00:07:59,021
Then von Neumann asks:

258
00:07:59,283 --> 00:07:59,933
but how do you know?

259
00:08:00,346 --> 00:08:01,258
And God says:

260
00:08:01,671 --> 00:08:03,233
I know where all the zeros are,

261
00:08:03,608 --> 00:08:04,646
and they're all on the line.

262
00:08:05,071 --> 00:08:06,408
That's not enough for von Neumann.

263
00:08:06,833 --> 00:08:09,333
He gets so agitated trying to understand why,

264
00:08:09,721 --> 00:08:11,271
that he ends up in the other place.

265
00:08:11,683 --> 00:08:12,458
Alex sums it up.

266
00:08:12,871 --> 00:08:13,983
Math is not the fact.

267
00:08:14,396 --> 00:08:14,946
It's the why.

268
00:08:15,358 --> 00:08:16,958
If you tell me something is true and

269
00:08:16,971 --> 00:08:18,133
I don't understand why,

270
00:08:18,558 --> 00:08:20,496
you have added zero to my knowledge.

271
00:08:20,908 --> 00:08:23,271
A proof sitting as ones and zeros in

272
00:08:23,283 --> 00:08:23,983
a data center,

273
00:08:24,358 --> 00:08:25,046
he argues,

274
00:08:25,433 --> 00:08:27,558
is not the value of pure mathematics.

275
00:08:27,971 --> 00:08:29,746
The value lives in human heads.

276
00:08:30,171 --> 00:08:33,233
That's why he doesn't believe AI destroys mathematics,

277
00:08:33,621 --> 00:08:35,258
even in a world where it could solve

278
00:08:35,421 --> 00:08:36,321
everything we can.

279
00:08:36,733 --> 00:08:37,846
And as Ankur puts it,

280
00:08:38,158 --> 00:08:39,471
when you fight with a good problem,

281
00:08:39,846 --> 00:08:40,696
it fights back.

282
00:08:41,121 --> 00:08:43,208
That fight is where understanding comes from.

283
00:08:43,712 --> 00:08:44,312
Part six.

284
00:08:44,725 --> 00:08:46,613
Advice for an anxious generation.

285
00:08:47,088 --> 00:08:49,575
Finally, Lex asked what everyone listening wanted to

286
00:08:49,588 --> 00:08:49,863
